<p class="guide-lead">
  Roughly sits at the meeting point of several bodies of work. This map offers starting passages and explains what each connection helps us ask.
</p>
<p>
  These thinkers disagree with one another. The connections below are interpretations for Roughly, rather than a single theory they all endorse. The
  <a href="https://github.com/bcooke/roughly/blob/main/vault/discovery/2026-11-03-knowledge-and-measurement.md">research notes</a>
  retain the scope, qualifications, and further reading.
</p>
<h2 id="reading-knowledge">What counts as knowledge?</h2>
<ul class="guide-reading-list">
  <li>
    <a href="https://classics.mit.edu/Plato/theatu.html">Plato · Theaetetus</a><p>
      The discussion of true judgment and an account, especially 201c–210b. It ends with difficulties, rather than a settled recipe for knowledge.
    </p>
  </li>
  <li>
    <a href="https://classics.mit.edu/Aristotle/posterior.1.i.html">Aristotle · Posterior Analytics</a><p>
      Book I distinguishes understanding terms, knowing facts, and explaining causes. A descriptive percentage alone does not explain a pattern.
    </p>
  </li>
  <li>
    <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/10616/pg10616.html">Locke · An Essay Concerning Human Understanding</a><p>
      Book IV, chapters XIV–XVI, on judgment, probability, and assent under fallible evidence.
    </p>
  </li>
  <li>
    <a href="https://barryfvaughan.org/text/philtext/hume/enquiry/4a.html">Hume · An Enquiry Concerning Human Understanding</a><p>
      Section IV separates matters of fact from relations of ideas. A formula and its empirical inputs have different kinds of justification.
    </p>
  </li>
  <li>
    <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/files/4280/4280-h/4280-h.htm">Kant · Critique of Pure Reason</a><p>
      The Introduction and discussion of phenomena and noumena examine the conditions of cognition. Missing data should not be confused with his limits on knowledge of things in themselves.
    </p>
  </li>
  <li>
    <a href="https://www.peirce.org/writings/p107.html">Peirce · The Fixation of Belief</a><p>
      Investigation constrained by reality differs from securing agreement through authority or tenacity. Consensus alone is not validation.
    </p>
  </li>
  <li>
    <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/5827/pg5827-images.html">Russell · The Problems of Philosophy</a><p>
      Chapters V and XII–XIV distinguish description, truth, and how we can know. A well-described target can still have an unknown value.
    </p>
  </li>
  <li>
    <a href="https://www.nationalacademies.org/read/18573/chapter/3">Searle · Theory of Mind and Darwin’s Legacy</a><p>
      The distinction between epistemic objectivity and ontological subjectivity helps explain objective investigation of subjective experience.
    </p>
  </li>
</ul>
<h2 id="reading-language">How do definitions get their meaning?</h2>
<ul class="guide-reading-list">
  <li>
    <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/files/5740/5740-pdf.pdf">Wittgenstein · Tractatus Logico-Philosophicus</a><p>
      Proposition 4.024 separates understanding a proposition from knowing its truth. His later <a href="https://ericgerlach.com/wp-content/uploads/2019/02/3f786-ludwig.wittgenstein.-.philosophical.investigations.pdf">Philosophical Investigations, §43</a>, examines meaning through use.
    </p>
  </li>
  <li>
    <a href="https://www.fitelson.org/confirmation/carnap_logical_foundations_of_probability.pdf">Carnap · Logical Foundations of Probability</a><p>
      Chapter I, §§2–3: explication makes an imprecise concept more exact, assessing similarity, exactness, fruitfulness, and simplicity. A useful model for developing Roughly’s definitions.
    </p>
  </li>
  <li>
    <a href="https://www.uhu.es/francisco.moreno/gii_mac/docs/Godel_paper_1931.pdf">Gödel · On Formally Undecidable Propositions</a><p>
      Theorem VI concerns proof in formal systems under specific assumptions. It does not turn incomplete survey evidence into a theorem about unknowability. This partial translation includes translator commentary.
    </p>
  </li>
</ul>
<h2 id="reading-measurement">What does a measurement establish?</h2>
<ul class="guide-reading-list">
  <li>
    <a href="https://www.marxists.org/reference/subject/philosophy/works/us/bridgman.htm">Bridgman · The Logic of Modern Physics</a><p>
      The opening discussion of length connects concepts to measurement operations. Roughly must still distinguish a target from any particular instrument.
    </p>
  </li>
  <li>
    <a href="https://psychclassics.yorku.ca/Cronbach/construct.htm">Cronbach and Meehl · Construct Validity in Psychological Tests</a><p>
      A proposed interpretation needs evidence. Accurately storing a response does not establish that it measures the intended trait.
    </p>
  </li>
  <li>
    <a href="https://stanford.edu/class/psych205/papers/Tversky-Kahneman-1981.pdf">Tversky and Kahneman · The Framing of Decisions and the Psychology of Choice</a><p>
      Preference reversals under alternative decision frames motivate care with wording and context, without proving an effect for every question.
    </p>
  </li>
</ul>
<h2 id="reading-statistics">Which population does a result describe?</h2>
<ul class="guide-reading-list">
  <li>
    <a href="https://www.stat.cmu.edu/~brian/905-2008/papers/neyman-1934-jrss.pdf">Neyman · The Representative Method</a><p>
      The 1934 paper treats sampling and estimation as explicit methodological choices.
    </p>
  </li>
  <li>
    <a href="https://aapor.org/wp-content/uploads/2022/11/NPS_TF_Report_Final_7_revised_FNL_6_22_13.pdf">AAPOR · Non-probability Sampling</a><p>
      The task-force report examines when inference from nonprobability samples can be useful and the assumptions it requires.
    </p>
  </li>
  <li>
    <a href="https://www.stats.uwo.ca/faculty/aim/2015/9938/articles/Robinson1950AmericanSociologicalReview.pdf">Robinson · Ecological Correlations and the Behavior of Individuals</a><p>
      Relationships between groups cannot generally substitute for relationships between individuals. Separate aggregate totals do not establish individual overlap.
    </p>
  </li>
  <li>
    <a href="https://www.census.gov/about/policies/quality/standards/standardd3.html">US Census Bureau · Nonsampling Error</a><p>
      Coverage, nonresponse, processing, and measurement provide distinct ways a result can go wrong.
    </p>
  </li>
  <li>
    <a href="https://database.ich.org/sites/default/files/E9-R1_Step4_Guideline_2019_1203.pdf">ICH · Estimands and Sensitivity Analysis</a><p>
      Sections A.3 and A.3.3 illustrate defining a target before selecting an estimator. The clinical-trial framework is an example, not Roughly’s complete model.
    </p>
  </li>
</ul>
<h2 id="reading-identification">What can the available evidence identify?</h2>
<p>
  <a href="https://www.cemmap.ac.uk/wp-content/legacy/forms/manskipaper.pdf">Charles Manski · Partial Identification of Probability Distributions</a>, introduction: evidence and assumptions can constrain a population quantity to a set of values, rather than identify a single answer.
</p>
<h2 id="reading-crowds">When does a crowd help?</h2>
<ul class="guide-reading-list">
  <li>
    <a href="https://gwern.net/doc/statistics/prediction/1907-galton.pdf">Galton · Vox Populi</a><p>
      A historical example of aggregated estimates of a common external quantity: an ox’s weight.
    </p>
  </li>
  <li>
    <a href="https://www.asecib.ase.ro/mps/TheWisdomOfCrowds-JamesSurowiecki.pdf">James Surowiecki · The Wisdom of Crowds</a><p>
      Chapter 1 emphasizes diversity, independence, decentralization, and aggregation. Combining guesses differs from collecting reports of individual lives.
    </p>
  </li>
  <li>
    <a href="https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3107299/">Lorenz and colleagues · Social Influence and Crowd Wisdom</a><p>
      A laboratory study in which social information could reduce diversity without improving collective accuracy. A reason to investigate exposure, rather than a universal rule against it.
    </p>
  </li>
  <li>
    <a href="https://www.fooledbyrandomness.com/glossary.pdf">Nassim Nicholas Taleb · The Black Swan glossary</a><p>
      Silent evidence, epistemic opacity, and the ludic fallacy direct attention to missing observations and assumptions hidden by tidy models.
    </p>
  </li>
  <li>
    <a href="https://www.natesilver.net/p/model-methodology-2024">Nate Silver · Model Methodology</a><p>
      A concrete example of exposing forecasting choices and revisions. Forecast probabilities and descriptive population shares answer different questions.
    </p>
  </li>
  <li>
    <a href="https://www.gapminder.org/factfulness-book/mistakes/">Gapminder · Factfulness Corrections</a><p>
      Public corrections include question wording and chart interpretation, illustrating that explanations and instruments need revision too.
    </p>
  </li>
</ul>
<h2 id="reading-data">How can statistical knowledge be shared?</h2>
<p>
  <a href="https://docs.datacommons.org/data_model.html">Data Commons</a>
  separates statistical variables from dated observations and their provenance.
  <a href="https://www.w3.org/TR/vocab-data-cube/">W3C’s RDF Data Cube</a>
  describes dimensions, measures, and attributes for published statistical observations. Both offer technical precedents for connected, reusable data.
</p>
<p>
  This is a starting map, centered on accessible Western philosophical and statistical sources. Further investigations should include other traditions of knowledge and testimony, Bayesian inference, classification, and the social consequences of measurement. Suggestions and corrections belong in the <a href="https://github.com/bcooke/roughly/blob/main/CONTRIBUTING.md">open project</a>.
</p>
